BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260424T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260424T140000
LOCATION:Online
SUMMARY:Identifying and Understanding Autism in Women: Assessment Through
  Intervention (Online)
DESCRIPTION:This presentation for mental health practitioners aims to dee
 pen understanding of how autism and related forms of neurodivergence pres
 ent in girls and women\, and to support more accurate\, ethical\, and aff
 irming identification and care.  Autism is a complex and multifaceted for
 m of neurodivergence\, encompassing a wide range of traits that can prese
 nt differently across individuals\, contexts\, and developmental stages. 
 The most widely-recognized presentation of autism is generally based on r
 esearch conducted on white\, middle- to upper-class males\, often emphasi
 zing conventionally "masculine" interests and behaviors.  As a result\, m
 uch of the diagnostic criteria for autism and ADHD in the DSM does not&#1
 60\;capture how neurodivergence presents in girls\, women\, transgender a
 nd nonbinary individuals\, and people of color.Many autistic females and 
 gender-diverse individuals are misdiagnosed\, overlooked\, or misundersto
 od in childhood\, with their traits minimized\, masked\, or misinterprete
 d as behavioral or emotional difficulties rather than recognized as neuro
 developmental differences.  Recent research finds that nearly 80% of auti
 stic females are undiagnosed as of the age of 18.  This presentation will
  explore the following:Neurodivergence in girls and women: How traits in 
 females both align with and diverge from DSM criteria\, including differe
 nces in cognitive and sensory processing\, executive functioning\, social
  cognition\, communication\, and the influence of gendered expectations o
 n identification    Masking and it's impact: What is it\, how does it dev
 elop\, and its long- and short- term consequences    Assessment and refer
 ral: Best practices\, including timing\, developmentally sensitive testin
 g\, ethical and equity-informed considerations\, and neurodiversity-affir
 ming language\, and Theory of Mind&#160\;    Treatment and Support: Nervo
 us system–informed\, strengths-based interventions that support unmaski
 ng and self-understanding\, prevent autistic burnout\, and promote sustai
 nable functioning through individualized\, neurodiversity-affirming treat
 ment plans      This workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Con
 tinuing Education requirement. Click here for more information (http://ww
 w.oregon.gov/oblpct/Pages/CCCE.aspx)Details &amp\; RegistrationRegistrati
 on for this workshop is now closedDate and Time: Friday\, April 24\, 2026
 \, 9 a.m.-2 p.m.\, PT\, OnlinePresenter: Ashley Taylor\, PsyDCost and Cre
 dits: $105\, includes 4 CEUs. $45 student rate. GSEC Alumni and Adjuncts 
 save 20%  No-cost registration available for GSEC Mental Health Clinical 
 Supervisors\, Faculty\, and Staff. Email cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclar
 k.ed) with your request to register.Accessibility: We are committed to ma
 king our events accessible to all needs and abilities. When registering\,
  please let us know your access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@
 lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu)&#160\;with questions.Please note: Disc
 ounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for current stude
 nts only and do not include continuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)About t
 he PresenterDr. Ashley Taylor is a licensed clinical psychologist in Cali
 fornia and Washington State\, author\, and founder of Wonder Tree Develop
 mental Psychology. She specializes in providing comprehensive evaluations
  through a reflective\, neurodiversity affirming lens. She has provided h
 igh quality care to those with neurodivergence for over 15 years. Dr. Tay
 lor lives in Southwest Washington with her husband and two boys. She has 
 recently become a yoga teacher and enjoys spending time with her family\,
  pets\, going hiking\, snowboarding\, and doing yoga. Wonder Tree Develop
 mental Psychology has locations in Vancouver\, Washington and Pasadena\, 
 California\, providing an excellent team of developmental psychologists\,
  occupational therapists\, and educational specialists that support devel
 opmental across the lifespan.New workshops and trainings are added to our
  calendar regularly. For the latest on professional development related t
 o your specific interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark
 .tfaforms.net/4735441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This present
 ation for mental health practitioners aims to deepen understanding of how
  autism and related forms of neurodivergence present in girls and women\,
  and to support more accurate\, ethical\, and affirming identification an
 d care.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Autism is a complex and multif
 aceted form of neurodivergence\, encompassing a wide range of traits that
  can present differently across individuals\, contexts\, and developmenta
 l stages. The most widely-recognized presentation of autism is generally 
 based on research conducted on white\, middle- to upper-class males\, oft
 en emphasizing conventionally "masculine" interests and behaviors.</p><p 
 style="line-height: 1.5\;">  As a result\, much of the diagnostic criteri
 a for autism and ADHD in the DSM does not&#160\;capture how neurodivergen
 ce presents in girls\, women\, transgender and nonbinary individuals\, an
 d people of color.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><span style="backgrou
 nd-color: transparent\;">Many autistic females and gender-diverse individ
 uals are misdiagnosed\, overlooked\, or misunderstood in childhood\, with
  their traits minimized\, masked\, or misinterpreted as behavioral or emo
 tional difficulties rather than recognized as neurodevelopmental differen
 ces.</span></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Recent research finds that
  nearly 80% of autistic females are undiagnosed as of the age of 18.</p><
 h4>  This presentation will explore the following:</h4><ul><li><p style="
 line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Neurodivergence in girls and women:</strong> 
 How traits in females both align with and diverge from DSM criteria\, inc
 luding differences in cognitive and sensory processing\, executive functi
 oning\, social cognition\, communication\, and the influence of gendered 
 expectations on identification    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5
 \;"><strong>Masking and it's impact:</strong> What is it\, how does it de
 velop\, and its long- and short- term consequences    </p></li><li><p sty
 le="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Assessment and referral:</strong> Best pr
 actices\, including timing\, developmentally sensitive testing\, ethical 
 and equity-informed considerations\, and neurodiversity-affirming languag
 e\, and Theory of Mind&#160\;    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\
 ;"><strong>Treatment and Support:</strong> Nervous system–informed\, st
 rengths-based interventions that support unmasking and self-understanding
 \, prevent autistic burnout\, and promote sustainable functioning through
  individualized\, neurodiversity-affirming treatment plans    </p></li></
 ul><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cult
 ural Competence Continuing Education requirement. <a href="http://www.ore
 gon.gov/oblpct/Pages/CCCE.aspx">Click here for more information</a></p><h
 r /><h2><strong>Details &amp\; Registration</strong></h2><h5 style="line-
 height: 1.5\;"><strong>Registration for this workshop is now closed</stro
 ng></h5><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Date and Time:</strong> Fri
 day\, April 24\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-2 p.m.\, PT\, Online</p><p style="line-hei
 ght: 1.5\;"><strong>Presenter:</strong> Ashley Taylor\, PsyD</p><p style=
 "line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Cost and Credits:</strong> $105\, includes 4
  CEUs. $45 student rate. GSEC Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%</p><p style="l
 ine-height: 1.5\;">  No-cost registration available for GSEC Mental Healt
 h Clinical Supervisors\, Faculty\, and Staff. Email <a href="mailto:cce@l
 clark.ed">cce@lclark.edu</a> with your request to register.</p><div id="l
 w_widget_30f0017c" class="lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blurb
 s lw_widget_id_7119 lw_widget_accessibility_statement lw_widget_paginatio
 n"><ul class="lw_widget_results lw_widget_results_blurbs"><li class="lw_i
 tem_1"><div class="lw_blurbs_body"><p class="p1" style="font-size: 15px\;
  line-height: 1.5\;"><em><strong>Accessibility:</strong> We are committed
  to making our events accessible to all needs and abilities. When registe
 ring\, please let us know your access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 o
 r <a href="mailto:cce@lclark.edu">cce@lclark.edu</a>&#160\;with questions
 .<br/></em></p><p class="p1" style="font-size: 15px\; line-height: 1.5\;"
 ><em><strong>Please note:</strong> Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x201
 9\; registrations are for current students only and <strong>do not</stron
 g> include continuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)</em></p></div></li></ul
 ></div><hr /><h2><strong>About the Presenter</strong></h2><p style="line-
 height: 1.6\;"><strong>Dr. Ashley Taylor</strong> is a licensed clinical 
 psychologist in California and Washington State\, author\, and founder of
  Wonder Tree Developmental Psychology. She specializes in providing compr
 ehensive evaluations through a reflective\, neurodiversity affirming lens
 . She has provided high quality care to those with neurodivergence for ov
 er 15 years. Dr. Taylor lives in Southwest Washington with her husband an
 d two boys. She has recently become a yoga teacher and enjoys spending ti
 me with her family\, pets\, going hiking\, snowboarding\, and doing yoga.
  Wonder Tree Developmental Psychology has locations in Vancouver\, Washin
 gton and Pasadena\, California\, providing an excellent team of developme
 ntal psychologists\, occupational therapists\, and educational specialist
 s that support developmental across the lifespan.</p><div id="lw_widget_4
 70b39ba" class="lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blurbs lw_widge
 t_id_3994 lw_widget_opt_in lw_widget_pagination"><ul class="lw_widget_res
 ults lw_widget_results_blurbs"><li class="lw_item_1"><div class="lw_blurb
 s_body"><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em><strong>New workshops and train
 ings are added to our calendar regularly.</strong> For the latest on prof
 essional development related to your specific interests\, <a href="https:
 //lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441">sign up for our mailing list!</a></em></p>
 </div></li></ul></div>
