DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260505T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260505T130000
LOCATION:Zoom
SUMMARY:Leadership Societies Exclusive CLE: Professor Yin on "Protecting 
 Judicial Independence From Populist Threats"
DESCRIPTION:"Protecting Judicial Independence From Populist Threats"  An 
 independent judiciary is a critical part of a free society. Yet\, populis
 t leaders such as Trump\, Orban\, and others have sought to or have actua
 lly weakened their judiciaries. It is one thing to declare the principle 
 that judges should be independent of other branches of government. It is 
 another to take steps to protect the independence of the judiciary from p
 opulist threats.  This presentation provides an overview of such threats 
 to judicial independence\, along with ways in which a liberal democracy m
 ight structure its government to provide maximum protection to its judges
  – and the ways in which those protections might nevertheless fail. Spe
 cifically\, the presentation covers (1) different threats that judges mig
 ht face\, from retaliation in the form of loss of job\, to personal harm\
 , to dilution of judicial votes\, (2) ways in which the U.S. Constitution
  and federal statutes respond to these potential threats\, and tactics th
 at populist movements might nevertheless use to overcome those structural
  safeguards\, and (3) the possibility that transnational courts\, such as
  the European Court of Human Rights\, might play a key role in backstoppi
 ng a nation's own laws\, though such transnational courts also face chall
 enges in fulfilling that role.  Professor Yin will speak for approximatel
 y 40 minutes\, followed by a q/a period. 1.0 General CLE (pending approva
 l). Event RSVP by invitation only.Professor Tung Yin: (https://www.lclark
 .edu/live/profiles/271-tung-yin-)  Tung Yin joined the Lewis &amp\; Clark
  Law School faculty in 2009. Before that\, he taught for seven years at T
 he University of Iowa College of Law\, where he was most recently profess
 or and Claire Ferguson Carlson Faculty Fellow\; and practiced law from 19
 98-2002 with Munger Tolles &amp\; Olson LLP in Los Angeles\, where he spe
 cialized in white-collar corporate criminal defense and employment law. H
 e clerked for the late Hon. Edward Rafeedie\, U.S. District Court for the
  Central District of California\, the late Hon. William J. Holloway\, U.S
 . Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\, and the Hon. J. Clifford Walla
 ce\, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit. While in law school at 
 the University of California\, Berkeley\, he was a Notes and Comments Edi
 tor of the California Law Review and a member of the Moot Court Board.  Y
 in's academic research focuses primarily on national security and terrori
 sm law and has ranged from legal issues arising out of indefinite militar
 y detention of suspected terrorists at Guantanamo Bay to race and religio
 n and the perception of terrorism\, to drone terrorism\, and more. His sc
 holarship has been cited in judicial opinions from the U.S. Court of Appe
 als for the Fifth and Ninth Circuits\, the Florida and Georgia Supreme Co
 urts\, and other lower state and federal trial courts. In 2025\, he publi
 shed National Security Lies\, a monograph examining presidential lies abo
 ut national security matters.  He frequently provides commentary for loca
 l and national media on high-profile criminal matters\, including news ou
 tlets such as The Wall Street Journal\, The New York Times\, The Los Ange
 les Times\, Associated Press\, The New York Daily News\, Bloomberg News\,
  The National Law Journal\, The Oregonian\, Bloomberg Radio\, Oregon Publ
 ic Broadcasting\, KEX News\, KXL News\, KPAM News\, "The Lars Larson Show
 \," and the local news affiliates for ABC\, CBS\, NBC\, and Fox.  His for
 eign languages include Mandarin (limited working proficiency) and Japanes
 e (elementary proficiency).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="font-weight: 400\;">  "Protecting 
 Judicial Independence From Populist Threats"</p><p style="font-weight: 40
 0\;">  An independent judiciary is a critical part of a free society. Yet
 \, populist leaders such as Trump\, Orban\, and others have sought to or 
 have actually weakened their judiciaries. It is one thing to declare the 
 principle that judges should be independent of other branches of governme
 nt. It is another to take steps to protect the independence of the judici
 ary from populist threats.</p><p>  This presentation provides an overview
  of such threats to judicial independence\, along with ways in which a li
 beral democracy might structure its government to provide maximum protect
 ion to its judges – and the ways in which those protections might never
 theless fail. Specifically\, the presentation covers (1) different threat
 s that judges might face\, from retaliation in the form of loss of job\, 
 to personal harm\, to dilution of judicial votes\, (2) ways in which the 
 U.S. Constitution and federal statutes respond to these potential threats
 \, and tactics that populist movements might nevertheless use to overcome
  those structural safeguards\, and (3) the possibility that transnational
  courts\, such as the European Court of Human Rights\, might play a key r
 ole in backstopping a nation's own laws\, though such transnational court
 s also face challenges in fulfilling that role.</p><p>  Professor Yin wil
 l speak for approximately 40 minutes\, followed by a q/a period. 1.0 Gene
 ral CLE (pending approval). Event RSVP by invitation only.</p><p><a href=
 "https://www.lclark.edu/live/profiles/271-tung-yin-" target="_blank" rel=
 "noopener">Professor Tung Yin:</a></p><p>  Tung Yin joined the Lewis &amp
 \; Clark Law School faculty in 2009. Before that\, he taught for seven ye
 ars at The University of Iowa College of Law\, where he was most recently
  professor and Claire Ferguson Carlson Faculty Fellow\; and practiced law
  from 1998-2002 with Munger Tolles &amp\; Olson LLP in Los Angeles\, wher
 e he specialized in white-collar corporate criminal defense and employmen
 t law. He clerked for the late Hon. Edward Rafeedie\, U.S. District Court
  for the Central District of California\, the late Hon. William J. Hollow
 ay\, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\, and the Hon. J. Cliffo
 rd Wallace\, U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit. While in law sc
 hool at the University of California\, Berkeley\, he was a Notes and Comm
 ents Editor of the <em>California Law Review</em> and a member of the Moo
 t Court Board.</p><p>  Yin's academic research focuses primarily on natio
 nal security and terrorism law and has ranged from legal issues arising o
 ut of indefinite military detention of suspected terrorists at Guantanamo
  Bay to race and religion and the perception of terrorism\, to drone terr
 orism\, and more. His scholarship has been cited in judicial opinions fro
 m the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth and Ninth Circuits\, the Florid
 a and Georgia Supreme Courts\, and other lower state and federal trial co
 urts. In 2025\, he published <em>National Security Lies</em>\, a monograp
 h examining presidential lies about national security matters.</p><p>  He
  frequently provides commentary for local and national media on high-prof
 ile criminal matters\, including news outlets such as <em>The Wall Street
  Journal\, The New York Times\, The Los Angeles Times\, Associated Press\
 , The New York Daily News\, Bloomberg News\, The National Law Journal</em
 >\, <em>The Oregonian</em>\, Bloomberg Radio\, Oregon Public Broadcasting
 \, KEX News\, KXL News\, KPAM News\, "The Lars Larson Show\," and the loc
 al news affiliates for ABC\, CBS\, NBC\, and Fox.</p><p>  His foreign lan
 guages include Mandarin (limited working proficiency) and Japanese (eleme
 ntary proficiency).</p>
UID:20260505T190000Z-390030@www.lclark.edu
