DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260518
LOCATION:Menucha Retreat and Conference Center\, 38711 E\, Historic Colum
 bia River Hwy\, Corbett\, OR
SUMMARY:Liberatory Leadership: Embracing Grief and Possibility While Buil
 ding Beloved Community (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:This Courage to Lead® retreat is designed to help participan
 ts renew their passion\, enthusiasm\, and commitment to themselves and th
 e communities they serve\, and is open to those identifying as women who 
 are in leadership—whether formally or informally.  This four-day offeri
 ng is for those seeking to hold and respond to the complexity of our time
 \, while finding rest and rejuvenation through practicing community toget
 her. We will meet during the fullness of spring when the abundance of lig
 ht and energy invites us to remember all that brought us to this moment. 
  As individuals and as leaders\, we can expand our capacity to be fully p
 resent to ourselves and our community\, and reclaim our power to meet our
  calling in these increasingly challenging times.&#160\;With courage\, co
 mpassion and creativity we will share practices to hold our current reali
 ties\, wherever we find ourselves.We will invite the beauty of this speci
 al land above the Columbia Gorge to be a teacher and guide.&#160\;Togethe
 r we will…Pause\, breathe\, embrace silence\, and disconnect from techn
 ology and our demanding pace of life  Reduce isolation and build communit
 ies of care that provide support in hard times\, and act as containers fo
 r liberatory innovations  Identify reflective practices that help us embr
 ace grief and build hope for the future of our communities  Deepen our sk
 ills to ask open-ended questions that help us and other to uncover inner 
 wisdom  Expand capacity to be fully present to ourselves and others in wa
 ys that affirm and heal  Strengthen our ability to hold paradox\, moving 
 beyond either-or thinking into holding opposing truths and complexity  Le
 arn and participate in a Clearness Committee (https://couragerenewal.org/
 library/the-clearness-committee-a-communal-approach-to-discernment/)\, de
 signed after a powerful problem-solving model from the 1600's    We are e
 xcited to have you join in retreat\, grounded in the Courage &amp\; Renew
 al® approach\, upon which Circles of Trust (http://couragerenewal.org/ap
 proach/)® is based\, designed to build a trustworthy space for telling o
 ur truths and listening to the truths of others.Retreat Details &amp\;&#1
 60\;RegistrationRegistration for this retreat is currently full. Add your
  name to the waitlist (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735424/?epid=a1aUY000
 0023cE5) to be notified if space becomes available.  &#160\;Date and Time
 : Sunday-Wednesday\, May 17-20\, 2026. The retreat will begin at 4 p.m. S
 unday and conclude at 11 a.m. on Wednesday.Location: Menucha Retreat and 
 Conference Center\, Corbett\, ORFacilitators: Anita Garcia Morales\; Dawn
  Montgomery\, EdD\; and Jen Wagley\, MEd\, MDivCost: Registration fees in
 clude three nights of lodging\, 9 meals (beginning Sunday dinner and endi
 ng Wednesday Breakfast)\, and all supplies\, snacks\, beverages\, and 18 
 PDUs or CEUs.Single Occupancy Room: $1\,195&#160\;  Shared Occupancy: $89
 5  Accessibility:We are committed to making our events accessible to all 
 needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access ne
 eds. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu with questions.Scholars
 hip Fund: We strive to keep our Courage retreats accessible to all who wi
 sh to participate. Please consider making a tax-deductible donation (http
 s://engage.lclark.edu/register/gsecgiving?sys:gift:fund=4dfb9f4e-7351-4d8
 4-91fe-531631c248c7&amp\;sys:gift:notes=Courage%20and%20Renewal%20Retreat
 s%20Scholarship%20Fund) to our scholarship fund.About the FacilitatorsAni
 ta Garcia Morales retired from Seattle Public Schools after 35 years of t
 eaching and learning from students\, coaching/mentoring educators\, and d
 esigning and facilitating DEIB sessions for all district employees. Throu
 gh 'Racing to Equity'\, a consulting group Anita co-founded and co-owns\,
  she facilitates anti-bias circles with community groups\, government age
 ncies\, students\, educators and works with immigrant families who are ju
 st learning to navigate the complexities of their newly adopted home. Ani
 ta is a Courage &amp\; Renewal Facilitator\, a Positive Discipline Certif
 ied Associate\, and a Class Action Certified Trainer. The common thread t
 hat runs through all that Anita does is her focus on social justice and e
 quity. Anita is on a journey of self-healing from the generational trauma
  that she\, like IBPOC across the country\, have inherited and continue t
 o experience. Anita also sees herself as a healer calling up her ancestra
 l gifts and gathers circles of folx on similar paths of healing and liber
 ation. In this work\, IBPOC center themselves in their quest to show up i
 n the world more authentically and unapologetically.Dawn Montgomery\, EdD
  is a change facilitator and insightful thought partner. She supports lea
 ders from a wide variety of fields\, including educators\, nonprofit exec
 utives\, and community administrators as they find their way in challengi
 ng times. Assisting humans in their discovery and reconnection to their d
 eepest longings has been a passion for many years. Her background in K-12
  and college education as teacher\, principal\, and district leader suppo
 rt her coaching practice and team facilitation. She believes transformati
 on is born when a circle of humans sit together to listen\, to speak thei
 r deepest truths and to share their wildest imaginations for the world. S
 he is expert at holding space for both individual leaders and teams as an
  executive coach\, conflict mediator\, equity-focused professional develo
 per and a certified Courage &amp\; Renewal facilitator. She is committed 
 to making a difference in the lives of individuals and their communities.
 Jen Wagley\, MEd\, M Div serves as Deputy Director of Houston in Action. 
 She formerly served as the founding Executive Director of Our Children Or
 egon\, an advocacy and research organization committed to improving child
  well-being. She brings two decades of experience in organizational leade
 rship\, grass-roots power building and comprehensive community developmen
 t and engagement. Jenifer is a facilitator with the Center for Courage &a
 mp\; Renewal. Her work with the Center has been key to her sustainability
  as a leader and has provided her the opportunity of supporting other lea
 ders across the country. Previously she served as the Deputy Director of 
 Avenue Community Development Corporation in Houston\, TX. Jenifer is driv
 en by a passion to create a more just\, healthy\, loving society where ev
 ery person/being has what they need to thrive. Jen is mom to three boys a
 nd loves to hike\, coach basketball and build friendships. Jenifer holds 
 two master's degrees from Baylor University and Truett Seminary.&#160\;Ne
 w workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. For the la
 test on professional development related to your specific interests\, sig
 n up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
