DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260728T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260728T100000
LOCATION:Online
SUMMARY:Supporting Ukrainians Through Trauma Informed\, Culturally Compet
 ent Care (Online)
DESCRIPTION:This workshop invites clinicians into a deeper understanding 
 of the historical and systemic forces shaping Ukrainian mental health. Pa
 rticipants will explore the lasting impacts of colonization\, cultural er
 asure\, forced assimilation\, and ongoing geopolitical trauma\, and how t
 hese experiences can show up in clinical settings.  Participants will gai
 n insight into how these factors influence symptom presentation\, help-se
 eking behaviors\, and engagement in care\, and how a fuller understanding
  of clients' lived experiences can support more accurate assessment and m
 ore effective\, compassionate clinical work.  Together we will consider t
 he roles of individual\, intergenerational\, and collective trauma\, alon
 g with culturally relevant dynamics related to language\, identity\, and 
 safety.  This workshop also provides a space to reflect on implicit bias\
 , microaggressions\, and cultural misunderstandings\, and how they can im
 pact treatment outcomes. Participants will develop strategies to address 
 these dynamics and apply inclusive\, equity-focused approaches in assessm
 ent and treatment planning.  Clinicians will leave the training equipped 
 with practical tools to deliver ethical\, effective\, and culturally resp
 onsive mental health care for Ukrainian clients.Following this workshop\,
  participants will have the ability to:      Describe historical and syst
 emic factors\, including cultural erasure\, affecting Ukrainian mental he
 alth\, and explain their impact on client care          Demonstrate traum
 a-informed and culturally competent interventions when working with Ukrai
 nian clients through case examples          Identify instances of implici
 t bias and microaggressions in clinical scenarios and develop strategies 
 to address them          Apply equity-focused approaches in treatment pla
 nning to ensure inclusive\, ethical\, and effective mental health care   
    This workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Continuing Educa
 tion requirement. Click here for more information (http://www.oregon.gov/
 oblpct/Pages/CCCE.aspx)Details &amp\; RegistrationDate and Time: Tuesday\
 , July 28\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-1:30 p.m. PT\, OnlineInstructors: Valentina Pis
 hchanskaya-Cayanan\, LMFT\, CIMHP\; and Yulia Brockdorf\, RD\, LD\, CDCES
 \, CST\, NCC\, LPC\, BC-ADM\, BC-TMH\, LMHC\, FAIHM\, LN\, CD\, ACS\, FAW
 MCost and Credits: $105\, includes 4 CEUs. GSEC Alumni\, Adjuncts\, and C
 linical Mental Health Supervisors save 20%. $50 student rate.&#160\;Regis
 ter Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002XZAj)Acce
 ssibility: We are committed to making our events accessible to all needs 
 and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access needs. C
 ontact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu)&#160\
 ;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; re
 gistrations are for current students only and do not include continuing e
 ducation credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorsValentina Pishchanskaya-Ca
 yanan\, LMFT\, CIMHP&#160\;is a Ukrainian licensed marriage and family th
 erapist with extensive experience in clinical practice\, higher education
 \, and nonprofit leadership. She is the owner of Cayanan Counseling and W
 ellness\, a virtual private practice serving clients across Oregon and Wa
 shington. Her practice is dedicated to providing compassionate\, cultural
 ly attuned care with specializations in anxiety\, trauma\, and significan
 t life adjustments. Valentina is EMDR-trained and integrates evidence-bas
 ed\, trauma-informed approaches to help clients navigate change\, build r
 esilience\, and foster lasting well-being.  As a tenured faculty counselo
 r at Clark College\, Valentina provides psychotherapy\, teaches\, and dev
 elops programs to support diverse and underserved populations\, including
  Ukrainian students. She is also the Co-Founder and Vice President of DAW
 N Inc.\, where she leads humanitarian and medical aid initiatives in Ukra
 ine and supports refugee integration locally.  Valentina has presented at
  several conferences\, including the Northwest Regional Equity Conference
 \, on the needs of Ukrainians and the importance of culturally responsive
 \, trauma-informed care. She has also spoken to policymakers\, healthcare
  providers\, and community leaders about the ongoing challenges faced by 
 Ukrainians and ways to strengthen systems of support both locally and abr
 oad.  Through her clinical\, educational\, and nonprofit work\, Valentina
  is recognized for advancing resilience\, equity\, and culturally informe
 d trauma care in both local and international contexts.Yulia Brockdorf\, 
 RD\, LD\, CDCES\, CST\, NCC\, LPC\, BC-ADM\, BC-TMH\, LMHC\, FAIHM\, LN\,
  CD\, ACS\, FAWM&#160\;is a Ukrainian-American trauma psychotherapist and
  registered dietitian specializing in treating complex trauma and cultura
 lly competent care. She is the developer of the first combat psychotherap
 y model designed for delivery in active combat zones and has co-authored 
 peer-reviewed publications on the psychological impact of drone warfare. 
 Ms. Brockdorf provides direct psychotherapy to Ukrainian defenders along 
 Ukraine's front lines and has trained mental health professionals\, psych
 iatry residents\, and healthcare providers on trauma-informed approaches 
 and working with Ukrainian populations.  Ms. Brockdorf developed the trau
 ma and mental health course module Connecting with Ukrainian Refugees: Me
 eting Their Needs with Better Care and Key Service for the Washington Sta
 te Department of Health and has lectured at universities and medical scho
 ols on culturally responsive care for displaced populations. Her work has
  been published in peer-reviewed literature and she has been featured on 
 Oregon's Think Out Loud and other media outlets. She previously served as
  Board President of the Oregon Board of Licensed Dietitians.  As presiden
 t and co-founder of DAWN\, Inc.\, Ms. Brockdorf has coordinated over $20 
 million in medical aid deliveries to Ukraine. She serves on the executive
  team of DefenseTech for Ukraine and advocates for U.S. support of Ukrain
 e at state and federal levels\, including meetings with NATO officials\, 
 representatives of the European Parliament\, and members of the Governmen
 t of Ukraine\, and champions both local and international efforts in glob
 al security.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regular
 ly. For the latest on professional development related to your specific i
 nterests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/473
 5441)
