</picture></picture>Valen
 tina Pishchanskaya-Cayanan\, LMFT\, CIMHP</strong>&#160\;is a Ukrainian l
 icensed marriage and family therapist with extensive experience in clinic
 al practice\, higher education\, and nonprofit leadership. She is the own
 er of Cayanan Counseling and Wellness\, a virtual private practice servin
 g clients across Oregon and Washington. Her practice is dedicated to prov
 iding compassionate\, culturally attuned care with specializations in anx
 iety\, trauma\, and significant life adjustments. Valentina is EMDR-train
 ed and integrates evidence-based\, trauma-informed approaches to help cli
 ents navigate change\, build resilience\, and foster lasting well-being.<
 /p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  As a tenured faculty counselor at Clar
 k College\, Valentina provides psychotherapy\, teaches\, and develops pro
 grams to support diverse and underserved populations\, including Ukrainia
 n students. She is also the Co-Founder and Vice President of DAWN Inc.\, 
 where she leads humanitarian and medical aid initiatives in Ukraine and s
 upports refugee integration locally.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  V
 alentina has presented at several conferences\, including the Northwest R
 egional Equity Conference\, on the needs of Ukrainians and the importance
  of culturally responsive\, trauma-informed care. She has also spoken to 
 policymakers\, healthcare providers\, and community leaders about the ong
 oing challenges faced by Ukrainians and ways to strengthen systems of sup
 port both locally and abroad.<br />  Through her clinical\, educational\,
  and nonprofit work\, Valentina is recognized for advancing resilience\, 
 equity\, and culturally informed trauma care in both local and internatio
 nal contexts.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong><picture class="lw
 _image lw_image112607  lw_align_right"><source type="image/webp" srcset="
 https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/91/width/200/height/200/11
