DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260813T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260813T100000
LOCATION:Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School\, York 107
SUMMARY:K-8 Vocabulary Instruction that Sticks (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:Research is clear that early vocabulary experiences directly 
 correlate to later academic achievements\, yet many of our students lack 
 sufficient vocabulary to successfully access grade level content.&#160\; 
  Through this hands-on workshop\, K-8 teachers\, paraeducators\, and scho
 ol leaders will identify high-leverage vocabulary strategies and learn wa
 ys to implement manageable shifts that foster deep\, lasting student lear
 ning.  Utilizing the new Oregon Early Literacy Standards\, the current Ad
 olescent Literacy Standards\, and high quality texts as anchors\, partici
 pants will learn effective and efficient vocabulary teaching methods that
  are rooted in the Science of Reading and the Science of Learning\, and a
 re complementary to any literacy curriculum.&#160\;  Participants will le
 arn to utilize low-lift/high-leverage vocabulary practices that are prove
 n to boost achievement for all students\, with an emphasis on multilingua
 l\, emergent bilingual\, and neurodiverse students.Through this workshop\
 , participants will learn:Why explicitly teaching sounds\, spellings\, me
 anings\, and uses of words in context is vital for a student's overall co
 mprehension  How to model new vocabulary through oral and written languag
 e\, prioritizing comprehensible input for all students  Effective ways to
  extend the importance of students' active processing through academic pe
 er-discussionHow to elevate interactive read alouds to emphasize academic
  language and complex vocabularyEvent Details &amp\;&#160\;RegistrationDa
 te and Time: Thursday\, August 13\, 2026\, 9 a.m-12 p.m.Location: Lewis &
 amp\; Clark Graduate School\, York 107Instructor:&#160\;Leigh VonDerahePD
 U Registration: $79\, includes 3 PDUs\, GSEC Alumni\, Adjuncts\, and Teac
 her Education Mentors and Supervisors save 20%Register Online (https://lc
 lark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002Vn1h)Accessibility: We are comm
 itted to making our events accessible to all needs and abilities. When re
 gistering\, please let us know your access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6
 040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu)&#160\;with questions.Please
  note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for cu
 rrent students only and do not include continuing education credit (CEU/P
 DUs)About the InstructorLeigh VonDerahe\, MEd has worked in education for
  20 years\, with roles that include elementary classroom teacher\, interv
 ention specialist\, mentor teacher\, and graduate school supervisor. Leig
 h is currently the director of the Reading Intervention Program at Lewis 
 &amp\; Clark Graduate School of Education &amp\; Counseling. She teaches 
 graduate level courses for the intervention program\, as well as the lite
 racy sequence in the Elementary MAT program for preservice teachers.New w
 orkshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. For the lates
 t on professional development related to your specific interests\, sign u
 p for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
