DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260827T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260827T130000
LOCATION:Online
SUMMARY:Ketamine-Assisted Psychotherapy for Trauma and Collective Healing
  (Online)
DESCRIPTION:This workshop introduces clinicians to an integrative model o
 f ketamine-assisted psychotherapy (KAP)\, an emerging treatment for traum
 a\, depression\, and other mental health conditions. Participants will ex
 plore KAP as a relational\, trauma-informed modality that facilitates acc
 ess to implicit trauma memories\, attachment patterns\, and internal part
 s often difficult to reach through traditional talk therapy.  Participant
 s will learn how ketamine functions not as a standalone treatment\, but a
 s a catalyst within a carefully held therapeutic process that includes pr
 eparation\, guided non-ordinary states\, and structured integration.  Eff
 ective KAP requires attention to the broader relational\, cultural\, and 
 sociopolitical forces shaping psychological suffering and healing. This w
 orkshop presents KAP as a holistic\, trauma-informed approach that engage
 s psychological and psychospiritual dimensions of healing while addressin
 g systemic oppression\, historical trauma\, environmental disconnection\,
  and other factors that impact KAP prep\, administration\, and integratio
 n sessions.  Through lecture\, case studies\, experiential exercises\, an
 d discussion\, participants will learn practical\, culturally responsive 
 frameworks for conducting KAP sessions with an emphasis on relational saf
 ety\, ethical care\, and socially conscious practice.Following this works
 hop\, participants will have an increased understanding of:The clinical m
 echanisms and therapeutic applications of KAP  Ethical and therapeutic be
 st practices for KAP sessions that focus on preparation and administratio
 n\, and integration thereafter  Internal Family Systems (IFS) concepts th
 at support clients working with internal parts during ketamine experience
 s and understand how attachment theory informs preparation\, dosing suppo
 rt\, and integration of KAP  Mindfulness practices to facilitate somatic 
 support and grounding for KAP support  Trauma-informed practices for supp
 orting clients during the ketamine administration appointments  Ethical c
 onsiderations\, safety protocols\, and cultural responsiveness in KAP  De
 tails &amp\; RegistrationDate: Thursday\, August 27\, 2026\, 9 a.m.- 1 p.
 m. PT\, OnlineInstructors: Elizabeth Hoke\, LMFT\, and Julio Iniguez\, LM
 FTDetails: $125\, includes 4 CEUs. Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%. $60 GSEC
  Student Rate.Register Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a
 1aUY000002bzpl)  Limited no-cost registrations for GSEC Clinical Mental H
 ealth Supervisors\, Faculty\, and Staff. Email cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce
 @lclark.edu).Accessibility: We are committed to making our events accessi
 ble to all needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know you
 r access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@
 lclark.edu)&#160\;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student
  Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for current students only and do not inc
 lude continuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorsElizabeth
  Hoke\, LMFT is the Clinical Director of Liberation Institute in Portland
  where she supervises and provides psychoeducational training and worksho
 ps. She specializes in working with addiction and trauma recovery\, using
  a mix of theoretical tools rooted in somatic psychology and Eastern phil
 osophies. Elizabeth co-facilitates ketamine assisted psychotherapy sessio
 ns at Rainfall Medicine. She has completed the 100-hour MDMA Therapy Trai
 ning program through the Multidisciplinary Association for Psychedelic St
 udies and co-created the Entheogen Assisted Psychotherapy and Integration
  Training Series for licensed professionals at Lewis &amp\; Clark college
  in the fall of 2021. Elizabeth is also a member of Weaving Bridges Natur
 al Fellowship and helped create a psilocybin facilitator training program
  to teach individuals interested in becoming psilocybin assisted facilita
 tors licensed by the state of Oregon and is a psilocybin facilitator work
 ing with PNW Integrative Center.Julio Iniguez\, LMFT is a licensed marria
 ge and family therapist in New Jersey and Oregon. He completed a Master's
  degree in Marriage\, Couple and Family Therapy from Lewis &amp\; Clark C
 ollege in 2014 and a bachelor's degree from the University of California\
 , Irvine in Cognitive Psychology in 2004. Julio has experience with direc
 t service counseling in agencies\, program development and management\, a
 s well as clinical supervision of direct counseling staff. He brings a sy
 stemic\, culturally-informed\, and trauma-informed perspective to the ser
 vices he provides as a therapist\, clinical supervisor\, program administ
 rator and educator. Julio is adjunct faculty at Lewis &amp\; Clark Gradua
 te School of Education and Counseling where he teaches about Psychedelic 
 Assisted Therapy. He currently practices in Bloomfield\, New Jersey where
  he works with individuals and couples providing somatic experiential the
 rapy and ketamine assisted therapy.New workshops and trainings are added 
 to our calendar regularly. For the latest on professional development rel
 ated to your specific interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://
 lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This worksho
 p introduces clinicians to an integrative model of ketamine-assisted psyc
 hotherapy (KAP)\, an emerging treatment for trauma\, depression\, and oth
 er mental health conditions. Participants will explore KAP as a relationa
 l\, trauma-informed modality that facilitates access to implicit trauma m
 emories\, attachment patterns\, and internal parts often difficult to rea
 ch through traditional talk therapy.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  P
 articipants will learn how ketamine functions not as a standalone treatme
 nt\, but as a catalyst within a carefully held therapeutic process that i
 ncludes preparation\, guided non-ordinary states\, and structured integra
 tion.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Effective KAP requires attention
  to the broader relational\, cultural\, and sociopolitical forces shaping
  psychological suffering and healing. This workshop presents KAP as a hol
 istic\, trauma-informed approach that engages psychological and psychospi
 ritual dimensions of healing while addressing systemic oppression\, histo
 rical trauma\, environmental disconnection\, and other factors that impac
 t KAP prep\, administration\, and integration sessions.</p><p style="line
 -height: 1.5\;">  Through lecture\, case studies\, experiential exercises
 \, and discussion\, participants will learn practical\, culturally respon
 sive frameworks for conducting KAP sessions with an emphasis on relationa
 l safety\, ethical care\, and socially conscious practice.</p><p style="l
 ine-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Following this workshop\, participants will ha
 ve an increased understanding of:</strong></p><ul><li style="line-height:
  1.5\;">The clinical mechanisms and therapeutic applications of KAP  </li
 ><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Ethical and therapeutic best practices fo
 r KAP sessions that focus on preparation and administration\, and integra
 tion thereafter  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Internal Family Syst
 ems (IFS) concepts that support clients working with internal parts durin
 g ketamine experiences and understand how attachment theory informs prepa
 ration\, dosing support\, and integration of KAP  </li><li style="line-he
 ight: 1.5\;">Mindfulness practices to facilitate somatic support and grou
 nding for KAP support  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Trauma-informe
 d practices for supporting clients during the ketamine administration app
 ointments  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Ethical considerations\, s
 afety protocols\, and cultural responsiveness in KAP  </li></ul><hr /><h2
 ><strong>Details &amp\; Registration</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 
 1.5\;"><strong>Date:</strong> Thursday\, August 27\, 2026\, 9 a.m.- 1 p.m
 . PT\, Online</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Instructors:</stro
 ng> Elizabeth Hoke\, LMFT\, and Julio Iniguez\, LMFT</p><p style="line-he
 ight: 1.5\;"><strong>Details:</strong> $125\, includes 4 CEUs. Alumni and
  Adjuncts save 20%. $60 GSEC Student Rate.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\
 ;"><strong><a class="btn-small-style-1" href="https://lclark.tfaforms.net
 /wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002bzpl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Register On
 line</a></strong></p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Limited no-cost regi
 strations for GSEC Clinical Mental Health Supervisors\, Faculty\, and Sta
 ff. Email <a href="mailto:cce@lclark.edu">cce@lclark.edu</a>.</p><div id=
 "lw_widget_30f0017c" class="lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blu
